CONC = [
("h1", "Conclusion"),
("p", "The volume began with a plain statement: Paul sets Adam and Christ side by side twice, and the two passages do not answer the same question. It has tried to show that from the text. Romans 5:12–21 asks how sin, death and righteousness came to men, and it answers with one man’s offence and one man’s righteousness, a gift greater than the harm, and a reign in life for those who receive. First Corinthians 15 asks how the dead are raised and with what body, and it answers with a seed, four pairs of contrasts, and a first man and a last Adam who are the heads of the earthy and of the heavenly."),
("p", "The two passages share names, an enemy and a final title, and they differ in the question, the vocabulary, the joining word, the things said of the men and the way each describes those who belong to them. “In Adam” and “in Christ” name two headships. The first reaches the whole line of the first man. The second reaches those who receive and those who are in Christ. Neither passage names Israel, or a covenant, or an assembly as the members of either head."),
("ctbl", "The position in one table.", [
    ("Question", "The position", "Chief passages"),
    ("What does Romans 5:12–21 ask?", "How sin, death and righteousness reached “all men”", "Rom 5:12, 18"),
    ("What does the sentence of 5:12 say?", "Sin entered by one man, death by sin passed to all men, and all have sinned; how the one man’s act stands to each person is not stated", "Rom 5:12–14"),
    ("In what is Adam a figure?", "In the shape: one man, many affected. The gift is not as the offence", "Rom 5:14–17"),
    ("Who is the second headship said to reach?", "Those who receive abundance of grace and of the gift of righteousness", "Rom 5:17"),
    ("What does 1 Corinthians 15 ask?", "How the dead are raised, and with what body", "1Cor 15:12, 35"),
    ("How are the two men named there?", "The first man Adam and the last Adam; the first man and the second man", "1Cor 15:45–47"),
    ("What is the joining word?", "“By” in Romans 5; “in” in 1 Corinthians 15", "Rom 5:12, 18; 1Cor 15:22"),
    ("Who belongs to each head in 1 Corinthians 15?", "The earthy to the first; the heavenly to the second", "1Cor 15:48–49"),
    ("What do the passages share?", "Two men, one enemy, one final title", "Rom 5:14, 21; 1Cor 15:26, 57"),
    ("Do the passages name Israel or a covenant?", "No", "Rom 5; 1Cor 15"),
  ], [0.30, 0.46, 0.24]),
("p", "The volume asks to be tested. A reader who disagrees may take any row of the table and look for the verse that overturns it. If the verse is there, the position must change. If it is not, the position stands on what the text says. In either case the reader will have read two of the weightiest passages in Paul’s letters with the right question in hand, and that is no small gain."),
("p", "The volume has tried to be fair to the readings it opposes. Matt Perman and Thomas Constable see something real in Romans 5: that the whole weight of the results falls on one act. They are right, and the volume has said so. Where they add a doctrine that the sentences do not state, the volume has declined to add it, and has said where its disagreement lies. A reader who holds the doctrine is asked only to see where the words stop. The One of whom both passages speak is Jesus Christ our Lord, and the reader who comes away holding the text more closely has gained what the volume set out to give."),
]

